// @emit-error captured variable not found in enclosing frame
// THE KNOWN LIMITATION OF THIS SLICE, pinned so it is a measured boundary rather
// than something a consumer discovers. `docs/internals/buffer-design.md` §B3/O7.
//
// A load width is callable at TOP LEVEL and inside a named function — but NOT
// inside a named function in a module that uses a function value anywhere, and
// NOT inside a lambda body. Capture analysis records every free identifier that
// is not locally bound, a module global, or a collected function; a declared
// intrinsic is none of those, so its NAME rides the capture list and resolution
// finds nothing to read it from.
//
// Censused over all four call positions for all ten memory intrinsic names —
// the seven new widths, `__load_i32__`, `__memory_size__`, `__memory_grow__` —
// the answer is uniform: 10/10 work at top level and in a plain named function,
// 10/10 fail in the two positions above. `__log__` and `__trap__` do NOT fail,
// because they sit on `isBuiltinFnName`'s exemption list in
// `compiler/emit_base.vl` and these names do not. That one list is the whole
// difference, and fixing it is S1 — deliberately NOT in this slice, because the
// list is owned elsewhere and O7 asks whether to widen it or to fix capture
// analysis positionally instead.
//
// What it costs today: a program can address, grow and read linear memory at
// every width from top level, which is what the raw-address port needs — but a
// `Buffer` METHOD (a named function wrapping an intrinsic) is unusable in any
// program containing one lambda. That is why no `Buffer` ships here.
function peekF32(a: i32): f32 { __load_f32__(a) }

const xs = [1, 2, 3]
const doubled = xs.map((v: i32) => v * 2)
print(doubled[0])
print(peekF32(0))
